# deploy

GENERATED by infralift (stack `app-foundry-hetzner`). Everything here is
rewritten when the project changes in infralift except `infra/overrides.tf`,
which is yours and is only written if it does not exist.

The app runs on one Hetzner `{{server_type}}` in `{{location}}` behind a Cloudflare
tunnel, declared in {{state_tool}} and shipped by {{ci_name}}.

| Host | What |
|---|---|
| `https://{{subdomain}}.{{zone_name}}` | production |
| `https://{{subdomain}}-preview-<slot>.{{zone_name}}` | one per open {{change_request}}, up to {{max_previews}} at once |
| `{{subdomain}}-ssh.{{zone_name}}` | SSH, only through Cloudflare Access |

```
deploy/
├── infra/            root module: providers, backend, settings, overrides
│   ├── generated/    the stack itself
│   └── overrides.tf  yours
{{#if backend_r2}}├── bootstrap/        R2 bucket for tofu state (run once)
{{/if}}└── box/              what runs on the server: compose, Caddy, scripts
```

## What it builds

- **Server:** firewall with no inbound rules. Everything arrives through the
  tunnel, which the box dials out to.
- **Data:** production Postgres on a separate {{data_volume_gb}} GB volume with delete
  protection, so the server can be rebuilt without losing data.
- **Access:** `/admin` and `/api/analytics` on production sit behind Cloudflare
  Access for `admin_emails`. Previews do not serve them at all.
- **Backups:** nightly `pg_dump` to R2, kept {{backup_retention_days}} days, and every dump is
  restored into a throwaway Postgres to prove it works.
- **CI wiring:** apply writes every secret and variable the workflows need into
  {{ci_store}}.

Anyone who can push a branch to this repository is effectively root on the
box: same-repo {{change_request}}s deploy with the deploy key. Forks get no secrets.

## Standing it up

{{ci_name}} plans and applies `infra/`, so nobody has to run `apply` on a laptop.

- A {{change_request}} that touches the stack gets a plan: what it would add, change
  and destroy, and the rendered plan{{#if delivery_github}}, in the summary of its `infra plan` check{{/if}}{{#if delivery_gitlab}}, in the merge request's Terraform widget and the
  `infra_plan` job log{{/if}}.
  It does not take the state lock and is never applied. A {{change_request}} from a
  fork gets no credentials, so it gets no plan.
- Merging to the default branch plans again, saves that plan, and applies
  exactly the saved plan in the `production` environment{{#if delivery_gitlab}} when someone runs the
  manual `infra_apply` job (or straight away with `INFRA_AUTO_APPLY=1`){{/if}}. If state moved
  in between, the apply refuses the plan rather than apply something nobody
  saw. Applies run one at a time.

### Once

{{#if backend_r2}}1. Create the state bucket `{{name}}-tfstate`, with the bootstrap module (the steps
   are at the top of `bootstrap/main.tf`) or by hand in the Cloudflare
   dashboard. This is the one step that runs outside CI.
{{/if}}{{#if backend_tfc}}1. Make the `{{tfc_workspace}}` workspace in `{{tfc_organization}}` CLI-driven (a VCS-connected
   workspace refuses applies from the CLI), and set the credentials in the
   table under "Locally" on it as sensitive workspace variables, with the
   values from `terraform.tfvars.example`. Plans and applies execute there.
{{/if}}2. {{#if delivery_github}}In Settings, Secrets and variables, Actions, add:{{/if}}{{#if delivery_gitlab}}In Settings, CI/CD, Variables, add (masked where GitLab allows it):{{/if}}

   | Name | Kind | What |
   |---|---|---|
{{#if backend_r2}}   | `HCLOUD_TOKEN` | secret | the Hetzner project |
   | `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` | secret | the scopes under "Locally" |
   | `INFRA_GIT_TOKEN` | secret | the git host token under "Locally", which writes {{ci_store}}; the pipeline's own token cannot |
   | `BACKUP_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `BACKUP_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | secret | R2 keys scoped to the `{{name}}-backups` bucket |
   | `TFSTATE_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `TFSTATE_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | secret | R2 keys scoped to the `{{name}}-tfstate` bucket |
   | `TF_STATE_PASSPHRASE` | secret | the state passphrase; long and random, since saved plans are encrypted with it too |
   | `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` | variable | completes `backend.r2.hcl` from its example, and `cloudflare_account_id` |
   | `ADMIN_EMAILS` | variable | `admin_emails`, as a list: `["you@example.com"]` |
{{/if}}{{#if backend_tfc}}   | `TF_API_TOKEN` | secret | a Terraform Cloud token that can queue and apply runs on `{{tfc_workspace}}` |
{{/if}}

3. Gate production.
{{#if delivery_github}}   In Settings, Environments, open `production` (create it if no deploy has run
   yet):
   - Required reviewers: add the people or teams who approve. `infra apply`
     then waits for one of them; the plan they approve is the `infra plan` job
     of the same run. `deploy.yml` uses the same environment, so they approve
     app deploys too.
   - Deployment branches: selected branches, `main` only, so a branch with
     an edited workflow cannot reach this environment's secrets.
   - Environment secrets: put the write tokens here, under the same names.
     Inside the apply job they win over the repository's, so the repository
     can keep read-only ones for plans: a read-only Hetzner token, a
     Cloudflare token with read in place of edit, Secrets and Variables read
     on `INFRA_GIT_TOKEN`, and state keys with object read. With Terraform
     Cloud state it is one token: plan access on the repository, apply access
     on the environment. With only repository secrets, plans and applies both
     use them.
{{/if}}{{#if delivery_gitlab}}   Give the read-only values above the environment scope `*` and the write
   values the scope `production`, protected: only `infra_apply` names that
   environment, and protected variables only reach protected branches. On
   Premium, also protect the `production` environment (Settings, CI/CD,
   Protected environments) and add approval rules. On any tier the manual job
   runs only on the default branch, and only someone allowed to merge into a
   protected branch can run it.
{{/if}}
Then merge the {{change_request}} that adds `deploy/`. The first apply creates the
server and writes {{ci_store}}.

Who can see what: plan output is in the job log and summary for anyone who can
read them, with sensitive values redacted; on a public repository that is
everyone, including the saved plan artifact (encrypted with the state
passphrase on R2, a run reference on Terraform Cloud). Plans on {{change_request}}s read state, so
anyone who can push a branch here can read state, which matches the rule
above that pushing a branch is root on the box. What the split keeps from them
is the write tokens.

### Locally, when you need to

Credentials come from the environment only:

| Variable | Scope |
|---|---|
| `HCLOUD_TOKEN` | the Hetzner project |
| `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` | account: Tunnel, Access apps and policies, Access service tokens, R2 (all edit); zone `{{zone_name}}`: DNS edit, zone read. No token management. |
{{#if delivery_github}}| `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_OWNER` | Actions secrets and variables on `{{github_repository}}` |
{{/if}}{{#if delivery_gitlab}}| `GITLAB_TOKEN` | CI/CD variables and the backup schedule on `{{gitlab_project}}` (a token with the `api` scope) |
{{/if}}| `TF_VAR_r2_backup_access_key_id`, `TF_VAR_r2_backup_secret_access_key` | R2 S3 keys scoped to the `{{name}}-backups` bucket |
{{#if backend_r2}}| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | R2 S3 keys scoped to the `{{name}}-tfstate` bucket |
| `TF_VAR_state_passphrase` | encrypts state; `tofu init` needs it too, and losing it makes the state unreadable |
{{/if}}

{{#if backend_r2}}1. `cd deploy/infra`, copy `backend.r2.hcl.example` and
   `terraform.tfvars.example` without the `.example` suffix and fill them in.
2. `tofu init -backend-config=backend.r2.hcl && tofu plan -out tfplan && tofu apply tfplan`
{{/if}}{{#if backend_tfc}}1. `cd deploy/infra && terraform login && terraform init`
2. `terraform plan`, or start a run from the workspace.
{{/if}}
A local apply takes the same state lock as CI, so the two cannot overlap.

After the first apply, push to the default branch{{#if delivery_github}}, or run `deploy.yml` by hand{{/if}}{{#if delivery_gitlab}}, or run a pipeline on it by hand{{/if}}. Every deploy job
is skipped until the infrastructure exists.

## Acceptance

- a push to the default branch deploys, and `/api/health` reports `"database":"ok"`
- a {{change_request}} gets a working preview link, and closing it frees the slot
{{#if delivery_github}}- `backup.yml` writes a dump and its `verify-restore` job passes
{{/if}}{{#if delivery_gitlab}}- the nightly schedule's `backup` job writes a dump and `verify_restore` passes (run it by hand with `BACKUP=1`)
{{/if}}

`box/test-local.sh` runs the server half of this in local Docker and in CI.
